  "query_preview": "..\n(Sunday morning through next Thursday)\nIssued at 220 PM EDT Fri Oct 2 2026\n\nThe heaviest of the rain will start off the long term period as a \nstationary front associated with a weak trough makes its way down \nacross the south that should be primed for rainfall. PW values could \nexceed two inches overnight Sunday and into Monday morning. The \nmodels have come into better agreement on where the heaviest precip \nis most likely to occur, but there is still some uncertainty as \nit largely depends on how far south the front makes it. At this \ntime, it looks like the heaviest axis of precip will be along and \nsouth of I-85. While we're expecting widespread 1-3\" in this area,\na few spots could see more than 4\" which could cause localized \nflash flooding as well as river flooding as the rain drags into \nMonday. \n\nThe front lingers across southern Georgia through the start of next \nwork week. There is some uncertainty as to how much rain the very \nsouthern counties of our CWA will get as we move into next week. \nForecast totals range from a half an inch to an additional two \ninches until the front is pushed off to the east on Wednesday. \n\nFor a majority of the area however, rain is expected to come to an \nend overnight Monday and into Tuesday morning. Dry air will settle \nin across much of the area north of a line from Columbus to \nAugusta with high temperatures in the mid to upper 70s. Overnight \nlows will fall into the 50s with 40's likely in the higher \nelevations of northern Georgia.",
